  "gotchas": [
    "Auth: one token server for both environments — the docs' Multi-Environment Integrations example sends either environment's credentials to https://idp.flutterwave.com/realms/flutterwave/protocol/openid-connect/token, and Authentication says \"This process applies to both the sandbox and production environments. Make sure to use the correct credentials for each environment to obtain a valid token.\" \"Each token is valid for **10 minutes**\"; \"We recommend refreshing the token at least one minute before it expires.\" Cache the token. Sandbox: \"We archive your test data after 30 days.\"",
    "Headers: send X-Idempotency-Key on this POST. Docs (Idempotency): \"For all `POST` endpoints, include an idempotency key in the request header (`X-Idempotency-Key`).\" The API reference doesn't list it for this endpoint; sending it is harmless and follows the guide. A reused key replays the first response (\"the response will include the header `X-Idempotency-Cache-Hit: true`\"), so reuse the key when retrying the same operation and use a new one for a new operation. X-Trace-Id is optional. For both: \"It must be a string using only alphanumeric ASCII characters.\" \"Its length should be between 12 and 255 characters.\" The idempotency page also says \"We recommend using a **UUID** string as your idempotency key\": strip the UUID's hyphens to satisfy both.",
    "The body is a union on currency: NGN, GHS, UGX → account{code, number}; GBP → account{type individual | corporate, code, number, name}; USD → account{code, number, country: 'NG'} (USD accounts at Nigerian banks only). Other currencies can't be resolved here. Open question (GBP): the account-type branches are keyed individual / corporate, but the individual branch's own default is 'personal'.",
    "Docs (Introduction): \"This step is important to ensure that your transfer is processed successfully to the correct recipient.\" Compare data.account_name with the name the user typed before creating the recipient.",
    "Sandbox example from the docs: account {code: \"044\", number: \"0690000040\"}. The sandbox transfer scenario account_verification_failed exists for transfers; no lookup-specific scenario is documented.",
    "Errors: {\"status\":\"failed\",\"error\":{\"type\",\"code\",\"message\",\"validation_errors\"}}; error.code is a string and 400s carry validation_errors[{field_name,message}]. Besides the generic codes (10400 REQUEST_NOT_VALID, 10401, 10403 FORBIDDEN, 10404 RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND, 10409 RESOURCE_CONFLICT, 10422 UNPROCESSABLE, 10500) the error table documents these for this endpoint: 250400 ACCOUNT_VERIFICATION_FAILED, 251400 INVALID_ACCOUNT, 252400 UNKNOWN_BANK_CODE. Branch on error.type / error.code, not on the HTTP status alone. Docs (Errors): \"The current limit is 500 API requests per minute.\" A 429 can come back as a non-JSON page (\"HTML error pages such as `Cloudflare 1015 responses`\"): parse error bodies defensively and back off exponentially with jitter."
  ],
  "open_questions": [
    "Token URL: the PHP sample of Authentication uses https://keycloak.dev-flutterwave.com/realms/flutterwave/protocol/openid-connect/token; its curl and JavaScript samples and the Multi-Environment example use https://idp.flutterwave.com/realms/flutterwave/protocol/openid-connect/token, which this spec follows."
